=\"p equals 240 left parenthesis one half right parenthesis Superscript r\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This equation gives the number of players eliminated at the end of round <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> for a competition in which there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"240\"><mn>240</mn></math>, players.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This equation gives the number of players eliminated at the end of round <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> for a competition in which there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"240\"><mn>240</mn></math>, players, and for which the number of players doubles, rather than decreases by half, after each round.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This equation gives the number of players eliminated at the end of round <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> for a competition in which the number of players doubles, rather than decreases by half, after each round.</p>","externalid":"2d05a911-7298-4d94-ad93-ec0fefdedb1d","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1776362727992,"updateDate":1776362727992}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727992, 1776362727992),
    ($SATQ$000259aa$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$022849-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A group of monarch butterflies migrated from Chicago, Illinois, to Michoac&aacute;n, Mexico, flying a total of 2,100&nbsp;miles. It took a single butterfly in the group 120&nbsp;days to travel this route one way. On average, how many miles did the butterfly travel per day?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.057</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.729</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">17.5</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">24</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is correct. If the butterfly traveled 2,100 miles in 120 days, then it traveled, on average, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/000259aa/img_000_61aefa2a.png" alt="2,100 miles over 120 days equals, 17 point 5"></span> miles per day.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is approximately the average amount of time, in days, it took the butterfly to fly one mile: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/000259aa/img_001_9e3f82cc.png" alt="120 days over 2,100 miles equals, 0 point 0 5 7"></span> days per mile. Choice B is incorrect and may result from an arithmetic error. Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of hours in a day rather than the number of miles flown per day.</p></p>
$SATQ$, true, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"000259aa","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"022849-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A group of monarch butterflies migrated from Chicago, Illinois, to Michoac&aacute;n, Mexico, flying a total of 2,100&nbsp;miles. It took a single butterfly in the group 120&nbsp;days to travel this route one way. On average, how many miles did the butterfly travel per day?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.057</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.729</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">17.5</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">24</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. If the butterfly traveled 2,100 miles in 120 days, then it traveled, on average, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/000259aa/img_000_61aefa2a.png\" alt=\"2,100 miles over 120 days equals, 17 point 5\"></span> miles per day.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is approximately the average amount of time, in days, it took the butterfly to fly one mile: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/000259aa/img_001_9e3f82cc.png\" alt=\"120 days over 2,100 miles equals, 0 point 0 5 7\"></span> days per mile. Choice B is incorrect and may result from an arithmetic error. Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of hours in a day rather than the number of miles flown per day.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":2,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959618,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"000259aa","skill_cd":"Q.A.","score_band_range_cd":1,"skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","createDate":1691007959618,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"022849-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"15a9b1a2-ab71-4d2c-b978-96998e9c25a2","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"022849-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A group of monarch butterflies migrated from Chicago, Illinois, to Michoac&aacute;n, Mexico, flying a total of 2,100&nbsp;miles. It took a single butterfly in the group 120&nbsp;days to travel this route one way. On average, how many miles did the butterfly travel per day?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.057</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.729</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">17.5</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">24</p>\n"}},"rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. If the butterfly traveled 2,100 miles in 120 days, then it traveled, on average, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2,100 miles over 120 days equals, 17 point 5\"></span> miles per day.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is approximately the average amount of time, in days, it took the butterfly to fly one mile: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"120 days over 2,100 miles equals, 0 point 0 5 7\"></span> days per mile. Choice B is incorrect and may result from an arithmetic error. Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of hours in a day rather than the number of miles flown per day.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959618,"updateDate":1691007959618,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959618, 1691007959618),
    ($SATQ$0108ac2d$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$029303-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.F.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inference from sample statistics and margin of error $SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">At a large high school, 300 students were selected at random and were asked in a survey about a menu change in the school cafeteria. All 300 students completed the survey. It was estimated that 38% of the students were in support of a menu change, with a margin of error of 5.5%. Which of the following is the best interpretation of the survey results?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is 38%.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is greater than 38%.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Plausible values of the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change are between 32.5% and 43.5%.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Plausible values of the number of the students at the school who support a menu&nbsp;change are between 295 and 305.</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that an estimated 38% of sampled students at the school were in support of a menu change, with a margin of error of 5.5%. It follows that the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is 38% plus or minus 5.5%. The lower bound of this estimation is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0108ac2d/img_000_08d3123c.png" alt="38 minus 5 point 5"></span>, or 32.5%. The upper bound of this estimation is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0108ac2d/img_001_c806af09.png" alt="38 plus 5 point 5"></span>, or 43.5%. Therefore, plausible values of the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change are between 32.5% and 43.5%.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the percent of the sampled students at the school who support a menu change. Choices B and D are incorrect and may result from misinterpreting the margin of error.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"0108ac2d","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"029303-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.F.","skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">At a large high school, 300 students were selected at random and were asked in a survey about a menu change in the school cafeteria. All 300 students completed the survey. It was estimated that 38% of the students were in support of a menu change, with a margin of error of 5.5%. Which of the following is the best interpretation of the survey results?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is 38%.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is greater than 38%.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Plausible values of the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change are between 32.5% and 43.5%.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Plausible values of the number of the students at the school who support a menu&nbsp;change are between 295 and 305.</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that an estimated 38% of sampled students at the school were in support of a menu change, with a margin of error of 5.5%. It follows that the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is 38% plus or minus 5.5%. The lower bound of this estimation is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0108ac2d/img_000_08d3123c.png\" alt=\"38 minus 5 point 5\"></span>, or 32.5%. The upper bound of this estimation is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0108ac2d/img_001_c806af09.png\" alt=\"38 plus 5 point 5\"></span>, or 43.5%. Therefore, plausible values of the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change are between 32.5% and 43.5%.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the percent of the sampled students at the school who support a menu change. Choices B and D are incorrect and may result from misinterpreting the margin of error.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":2,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959624,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"0108ac2d","skill_cd":"Q.F.","score_band_range_cd":2,"skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","createDate":1691007959624,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"029303-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"80094348-aed9-4733-b3a1-834003f0fac6","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"029303-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">At a large high school, 300 students were selected at random and were asked in a survey about a menu change in the school cafeteria. All 300 students completed the survey. It was estimated that 38% of the students were in support of a menu change, with a margin of error of 5.5%. Which of the following is the best interpretation of the survey results?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is 38%.</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is greater than 38%.</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Plausible values of the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change are between 32.5% and 43.5%.</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Plausible values of the number of the students at the school who support a menu&nbsp;change are between 295 and 305.</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"c","rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that an estimated 38% of sampled students at the school were in support of a menu change, with a margin of error of 5.5%. It follows that the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change is 38% plus or minus 5.5%. The lower bound of this estimation is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"38 minus 5 point 5\"></span>, or 32.5%. The upper bound of this estimation is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"38 plus 5 point 5\"></span>, or 43.5%. Therefore, plausible values of the percent of the students at the school who support a menu change are between 32.5% and 43.5%.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the percent of the sampled students at the school who support a menu change. Choices B and D are incorrect and may result from misinterpreting the margin of error.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959624,"updateDate":1691007959624}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959624, 1691007959624),
    ($SATQ$014c47ab$SATQ$, $SATQ$3bdb04eb-289a-4c97-b759-9893b1508252$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Probability and conditional probability$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<figure class="table"><table border="1">
<thead>
<tr style="text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;">
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;">&nbsp;</td>
<th style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;" scope="col">Site A</th>
<th style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;" scope="col">Site B</th>
<th style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;" scope="col">Total</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr style="height: 22.3906px;">
<th style="width: 22.7442%; height: 22.3906px; text-align: left;" scope="row">Tulip</th>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="35"><mn>35</mn>
</math></td>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="15"><mn>15</mn>
</math></td>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="50"><mn>50</mn>
</math></td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 22.3906px;">
<th style="width: 22.7442%; height: 22.3906px; text-align: left;" scope="row">Daffodil</th>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="31"><mn>31</mn>
</math></td>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="21"><mn>21</mn>
</math></td>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="52"><mn>52</mn>
</math></td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 22.3906px;">
<th style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;" scope="row">Total</th>
<td style="width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;"><math alttext="66"><mn>66</mn>
