able shows the distribution of two types of flowers at two different sites. If a flower represented in the table is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a flower from site A, given that the flower is a tulip? (Express your answer as a decimal or fraction, not as a percent.)</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["0.7","7/10"],"correct_answer":["0.7","7/10"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 35 Over 50 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>35</mn><mn>50</mn></mfrac></math>. Based on the table, there are a total of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn></math> tulips, and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"35\"><mn>35</mn></math> of these tulips are from site A. The probability of selecting at random a flower from site A, given that the flower is a tulip, is equal to the number of tulips from site A divided by the total number of tulips, which can be written as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 35 Over 50 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>35</mn><mn>50</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"seven tenths\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 35/50, 7/10, and .7&nbsp;are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017717,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"014c47ab","skill_cd":"Q.E.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","createDate":1755117017717,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"","external_id":"3bdb04eb-289a-4c97-b759-9893b1508252","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"b5aee5f6-6347-4f7f-986b-1b5d3c8c34a4","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<figure class=\"table\"><table border=\"1\">\n<thead>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\">&nbsp;</td>\n<th style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\" scope=\"col\">Site A</th>\n<th style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\" scope=\"col\">Site B</th>\n<th style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\" scope=\"col\">Total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3906px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 22.7442%; height: 22.3906px; text-align: left;\" scope=\"row\">Tulip</th>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"35\"><mn>35</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3906px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 22.7442%; height: 22.3906px; text-align: left;\" scope=\"row\">Daffodil</th>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"31\"><mn>31</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"21\"><mn>21</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"52\"><mn>52</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3906px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\" scope=\"row\">Total</th>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"66\"><mn>66</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"36\"><mn>36</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7442%; text-align: center; height: 22.3906px;\"><math alttext=\"102\"><mn>102</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The table shows the distribution of two types of flowers at two different sites. If a flower represented in the table is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a flower from site A, given that the flower is a tulip? (Express your answer as a decimal or fraction, not as a percent.)</p>","keys":["0.7","7/10"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 35 Over 50 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>35</mn><mn>50</mn></mfrac></math>. Based on the table, there are a total of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn></math> tulips, and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"35\"><mn>35</mn></math> of these tulips are from site A. The probability of selecting at random a flower from site A, given that the flower is a tulip, is equal to the number of tulips from site A divided by the total number of tulips, which can be written as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 35 Over 50 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>35</mn><mn>50</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"seven tenths\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 35/50, 7/10, and .7&nbsp;are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","externalid":"3bdb04eb-289a-4c97-b759-9893b1508252","correct_answer":["0.7","7/10"]},"createDate":1755117017717,"updateDate":1755117017717}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017717, 1755117017717),
    ($SATQ$022e9894$SATQ$, $SATQ$9d08821e-6f03-46fa-b7f1-5b1d8c3a6d3f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Percentages$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>An insurance company offers a series of three information sessions. <math alttext="1,250"><mn>1,250</mn>
</math> people attended the first information session. <math alttext="72 percent sign"><mn>72</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the people who attended the first information session attended the second information session, and <math alttext="36 percent sign"><mn>36</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the people who attended the first and second information sessions attended the third information session. How many people attended all three information sessions?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["324"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["324"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="324"><mn>324</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1,250"><mn>1,250</mn></math> people attended the first information session, and that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="72 percent sign"><mn>72</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the people who attended the first information session attended the second information session. Therefore, the number of people who attended the first and second information sessions can be found by calculating <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="72 percent sign"><mn>72</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1,250"><mn>1,250</mn></math>, which is equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1 comma 250 left parenthesis StartFraction 72 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>250</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mn>72</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="900"><mn>900</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s also given that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="36 percent sign"><mn>36</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the people who attended the first and second information sessions attended the third information session. Since <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="900"><mn>900</mn></math> people attended the first and second information sessions, the number of people who attended the first, second, and third information sessions can be found by calculating <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="36 percent sign"><mn>36</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="900"><mn>900</mn></math>, which is equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="900 left parenthesis StartFraction 36 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mn>900</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mn>36</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="324"><mn>324</mn></math>. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="324"><mn>324</mn></math> people attended all three information sessions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"022e9894","external_id":"9d08821e-6f03-46fa-b7f1-5b1d8c3a6d3f","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"2f2e95cb-338b-4538-950c-4286560dbfd9","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.B.","skill_desc":"Percentages","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>An insurance company offers a series of three information sessions. <math alttext=\"1,250\"><mn>1,250</mn>\n</math> people attended the first information session. <math alttext=\"72 percent sign\"><mn>72</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the people who attended the first information session attended the second information session, and <math alttext=\"36 percent sign\"><mn>36</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the people who attended the first and second information sessions attended the third information session. How many people attended all three information sessions?</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["324"],"correct_answer":["324"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3